      "nets": ["VMOT", "NET0115", "GND", "+3V3"],
      "observed": "R6 and R23 are both 50 kΩ in a divider from VMOT to GND, with midpoint NET0115 feeding ADS1115 U5 AIN0. U5 is powered from +3V3, so the divider applies half of VMOT to the ADC input.",
      "expected": "The divider ratio should keep AIN0 within the ADS1115 input range for the maximum VMOT voltage. With a 3.3 V supply, a 50/50 divider exceeds the input range whenever VMOT is above approximately 6.6 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "Power Control / ADC area, page 1: R6 “50k 0.1%” connects to VMOT; R23 “50k 0.1%” connects from NET0115 to GND; NET0115 is shown at U5 AIN0, and U5 VDD is connected to +3V3."
        },
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "Power over Ethernet area, page 1: the circuit is labeled as feeding VMOT and includes a “28V / 500mW” protection device."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "For VMOT above 6.6 V, AIN0 is driven beyond the ADS1115 supply range. The ADC reading will be invalid or clamped, and the input may be stressed; this undermines the input-voltage sensing function.",
      "confidence": 0.78
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The VMOT sensing divider uses equal 50 kΩ resistors, so it sends half of VMOT to an ADS1115 powered at 3.3 V. That exceeds the ADC input range for plausible VMOT levels indicated by the schematic’s higher-voltage circuitry."
}

